::ENG4_U06_L01_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which oral response order is strongest for listeners? {
~A. Evidence, unrelated detail, focus, conclusion.#Incorrect. The listener needs the focus before evidence.
=B. Focus, evidence, explanation, conclusion.#Correct. This order helps listeners follow the point and support.
~C. Conclusion, evidence, unrelated detail, focus.#Incorrect. This order is confusing.
~D. Unrelated detail, evidence, conclusion, focus.#Incorrect. This does not support clear oral communication.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source note: Workers with unreliable transportation are more likely to miss scheduled shifts. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Workers with reliable transportation miss more shifts.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~B. Transportation has no effect on work schedules.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source note.
~C. Workers should avoid all transportation.#Incorrect. This adds an unsupported idea.
=D. Unreliable transportation can make workers miss scheduled shifts.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which sentence uses academic vocabulary effectively in an oral response? {
=A. The evidence is credible because it comes from a local workforce report.#Correct. Credible is used accurately.
~B. The thing is good because stuff happens.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~C. Evidence is a word in the presentation.#Incorrect. This does not use academic vocabulary meaningfully.
~D. The claim is claimy and has evidence things.#Incorrect. This is not academic language.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which spoken sentence is clearest and most conventional? {
~A. Evidence supports claim because report.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
~B. The evidence supports the claim it comes from report.#Incorrect. This is a run-on sentence.
=C. The evidence supports the claim because it comes from a credible report.#Correct. This is clear and conventional.
~D. Because credible report evidence.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which statement best explains why focus matters in oral communication? {
~A. Focus matters only in writing, not speaking.#Incorrect. Focus is essential in oral communication.
=B. A clear focus helps listeners understand how each piece of evidence supports the main point.#Correct. Focus guides listener understanding.
~C. Focus means adding as many topics as possible.#Incorrect. Focus narrows the message.
~D. Focus replaces evidence.#Incorrect. Focus and evidence work together.
}
